course: dictionary
lesson: 2499
title: Debater: arguing with reasons
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A debater is a person who takes part in a debate. A debate is a formal argument, where speakers give reasons for an idea and reasons against it. Context helps here too: «a skilled debater» is someone who argues a point clearly and well.
explain: In school, debaters join the debating team or a debate club. They speak for or against a motion, such as «Homework should be banned». A judge listens to both sides and decides who argued better.
explain: Look at this sentence: «Politicians on all sides use straw men; a fair debater first repeats the real idea, then says why they disagree.» A fair debater repeats the real idea before arguing against it, so everyone knows exactly what is being discussed. A straw man is a weak copy of someone's idea, made up so it is easy to knock down.
fact: A debater is a person who takes part in a debate.
fact: A debate is a formal argument where speakers give reasons for and against an idea.
fact: «A skilled debater» is someone who argues a point clearly, with good reasons.
fact: Debaters join «the debating team» and speak for or against a motion, such as «Homework should be banned».
fact: A straw man is a copy of someone's idea that has been made weaker, so it is easy to knock down.
exercise: What is a debater? | *A person who takes part in a debate, giving reasons for and against an idea | A person who always agrees with everyone | A person who sits and listens without ever speaking | A judge who picks the winner
exercise: In a debate, what does «for and against» mean? | Shouting at the same time as everyone else | *Giving reasons both supporting and opposing an idea | Arguing about the weather | Agreeing with the judge only
exercise: «A fair debater first repeats the real idea, then says why they disagree.» Find and copy one word that means a person who argues. | judge | politicians | *debater | sides
test: Ruby says, «She is a skilled debater.» What does skilled suggest? | Loud and rude to everyone | Tired of talking all day | Lost in the argument | *Good at arguing a point clearly with reasons
test: A fair debater repeats the real idea before disagreeing. Why do they do this? | *So everyone knows exactly what is being argued | So the debate ends quickly | So the judge can go home early | So they can make the idea look silly
exam: Which of these is a straw man? | Repeating someone's idea exactly as they said it | A motion chosen by the judge | *A weak copy of someone's idea, made up so it is easy to knock down | A reason given for an idea
